# seq: seq6-alias-create
# desc: alias-creation as bind-mount-style aliasing preview (§3.23).
#       Real bind-mount requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN + mount(2); this sequence
#       uses symlink as a portable, in-userland aliasing primitive that
#       exercises the same seal-follows-inode invariant.
#       Step 1: actor reads sealed secret (ALLOW per actor list).
#       Step 2: outsider attempts open-via-symlink through the existing alias
#               that resolves to ME19_SECRET → DENY (resolved inode is sealed
#               full actor=a1; b1 not in actor list).
#       Step 3: outsider attempts to WRITE the sealed file by absolute
#               path → DENY (same inode; path irrelevant). (v0 has no
#               read-restriction; only write-class ops surface DENY.)
#       The witness: alias does NOT bypass the seal — neither the original
#       path nor any user-created alias matters; only (dev,ino) does.
me21_step 1 a1 open-ro          "$ME19_SECRET" ALLOW expected-ALLOW-actor-reads-sealed-secret
me21_step 2 b1 open-via-symlink "$ME19_ALIAS"  DENY  expected-DENY-outsider-via-alias-write-to-sealed-secret
me21_step 3 b1 write            "$ME19_SECRET" DENY  expected-DENY-outsider-direct-path-write
